{"content":[{"c":["MALACHI, Book of"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Last prophetic book of the Jewish canon; last book of the Old Testament."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["Preview"],"sourceClass":"h2-preview","t":"p"},{"c":["• ",{"href":"#Author","hrefKind":"unclassified","t":"link","v":"Author"}],"sourceClass":"preview-list-first","t":"p"},{"c":["• ",{"href":"#Background","hrefKind":"unclassified","t":"link","v":"Background"}],"sourceClass":"preview-list","t":"p"},{"c":["• ",{"href":"#Date","hrefKind":"unclassified","t":"link","v":"Date"}],"sourceClass":"preview-list","t":"p"},{"c":["• ",{"href":"#PurposeandTheology","hrefKind":"unclassified","t":"link","v":"Purpose and Theology"}],"sourceClass":"preview-list","t":"p"},{"c":["• ",{"href":"#Content","hrefKind":"unclassified","t":"link","v":"Content"}],"sourceClass":"preview-list","t":"p"},{"c":["Author"],"sourceClass":"h2","t":"p"},{"c":["The name Malachi means “my messenger” or “messenger of the Lord.” Since the word appears in ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.1","r":"Mal.3.1","rawR":"Mal.3.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.1","value":"Mal.3.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:1"},", some scholars think that it is not a proper name at all and does not provide the name of the author of the book. According to one ancient tradition, the “messenger” was Ezra, the priest responsible for the books of Ezra and Nehemiah. Yet it would be most unusual for the Jews to preserve a prophetic book without explicitly attaching to it the name of the author. All of the other major and minor prophets—including Obadiah—are named after a particular prophet. Moreover, “messenger of the Lord” would be a most appropriate name for a prophet (cf. ",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.36.15-16","r":"2Chr.36.15-16","rawR":"2Chr.36.15-16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.36.15-16","value":"2Chr.36.15-16"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Chr 36:15-16"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Hagg.1.13","r":"Hagg.1.13","rawR":"Hagg.1.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Hagg.1.13","value":"Hagg.1.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"Hg 1:13"},")."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["Background"],"sourceClass":"h2","t":"p"},{"c":["During the fifth century ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},", the struggling Jewish community in Judah was greatly assisted by the return of Ezra and Nehemiah. In 458 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"}," Ezra was encouraged by King Artaxerxes of Persia to lead a group of exiles back to Jerusalem and to institute religious reform. About 13 years later, in 445 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},", a high-ranking government official named Nehemiah was allowed to go to Jerusalem to rebuild the city walls, a task he accomplished in 52 days (",{"href":"?bref=Neh.6.15","r":"Neh.6.15","rawR":"Neh.6.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Neh.6.15","value":"Neh.6.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"Neh 6:15"},"). As governor, Nehemiah led the people in a financial reform that provided for the poor and encouraged tithing to support the priests and Levites (",{"href":"?bref=Neh.5.2-13","r":"Neh.5.2-13","rawR":"Neh.5.2-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Neh.5.2-13","value":"Neh.5.2-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:2-13"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Neh.10.35-39","r":"Neh.10.35-39","rawR":"Neh.10.35-39","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Neh.10.35-39","value":"Neh.10.35-39"}],"t":"ref","v":"10:35-39"},"). Like Ezra, Nehemiah urged the people to keep the Sabbath and avoid intermarrying with pagan neighbors. After a 12-year term, Nehemiah returned to Persia and the spiritual condition of Judah deteriorated. Perhaps discouraged by their lack of political power, tithing became sporadic, the Sabbath was not kept, intermarriage was common, and even the priests could not be trusted. When Nehemiah came back to Jerusalem sometime later, he had to take firm action to straighten out the situation (",{"href":"?bref=Neh.13.6-31","r":"Neh.13.6-31","rawR":"Neh.13.6-31","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Neh.13.6-31","value":"Neh.13.6-31"}],"t":"ref","v":"13:6-31"},")."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["Date"],"sourceClass":"h2","t":"p"},{"c":["Since Malachi had to deal with the same sins mentioned in ",{"href":"?bref=Neh.13.1-31","r":"Neh.13.1-31","rawR":"Neh.13.1-31","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Neh.13.1-31","value":"Neh.13.1-31"}],"t":"ref","v":"Nehemiah 13"}," (see ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.1.6-14","r":"Mal.1.6-14","rawR":"Mal.1.6-14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.1.6-14","value":"Mal.1.6-14"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mal 1:6-14"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.2.14-16","r":"Mal.2.14-16","rawR":"Mal.2.14-16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.2.14-16","value":"Mal.2.14-16"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:14-16"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.8-11","r":"Mal.3.8-11","rawR":"Mal.3.8-11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.8-11","value":"Mal.3.8-11"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:8-11"},"), it is likely that the prophet ministered either during Nehemiah’s second term as governor or in the years just before his return. The reference to “the governor” in ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.1.8","r":"Mal.1.8","rawR":"Mal.1.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.1.8","value":"Mal.1.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"Malachi 1:8"}," implies that someone other than Nehemiah was in office, so it may be best to place Malachi just after 433 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},", the year Nehemiah had returned to Persia."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["Purpose and Theology"],"sourceClass":"h2","t":"p"},{"c":["Malachi was written to shake the people of Judah from their spiritual lethargy and to warn them that judgment was coming unless they repented. The people doubted God’s love (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.1.2","r":"Mal.1.2","rawR":"Mal.1.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.1.2","value":"Mal.1.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:2"},") and justice (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.2.17","r":"Mal.2.17","rawR":"Mal.2.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.2.17","value":"Mal.2.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:17"},") and did not take his commands seriously (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.1.6","r":"Mal.1.6","rawR":"Mal.1.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.1.6","value":"Mal.1.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:6"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.14-18","r":"Mal.3.14-18","rawR":"Mal.3.14-18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.14-18","value":"Mal.3.14-18"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:14-18"},"). Yet God was “a great King” (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.1.14","r":"Mal.1.14","rawR":"Mal.1.14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.1.14","value":"Mal.1.14"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:14"},") with a great name that was to be feared even beyond the border of Israel (vv ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.1.5,11","r":"Mal.1.5,11","rawR":"Mal.1.5,11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.1.5,11","value":"Mal.1.5,11"}],"t":"ref","v":"5, 11"},"). Malachi repeatedly urged both the priests and the people to revere God and give him the honor he deserved. God was Israel’s Father and Creator (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.2.10","r":"Mal.2.10","rawR":"Mal.2.10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.2.10","value":"Mal.2.10"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:10"},"), but the nation showed contempt for his name (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.1.6","r":"Mal.1.6","rawR":"Mal.1.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.1.6","value":"Mal.1.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:6"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.5","r":"Mal.3.5","rawR":"Mal.3.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.5","value":"Mal.3.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:5"},"). In response to this contempt, God would send his messenger to announce the Day of the Lord (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.1","r":"Mal.3.1","rawR":"Mal.3.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.1","value":"Mal.3.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:1"},"). John the Baptist did call the nation to repentance, and Christ came to cleanse the temple (",{"href":"?bref=John.2.14-15","r":"John.2.14-15","rawR":"John.2.14-15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"John.2.14-15","value":"John.2.14-15"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jn 2:14-15"},") and to establish the covenant (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.1-2","r":"Mal.3.1-2","rawR":"Mal.3.1-2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.1-2","value":"Mal.3.1-2"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mal 3:1-2"},"). Most of the work of refining and purifying will take place at the Second Coming, when Christ returns to purify his people (cf. vv ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.2-4","r":"Mal.3.2-4","rawR":"Mal.3.2-4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.2-4","value":"Mal.3.2-4"}],"t":"ref","v":"2-4"},") and judge the wicked (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.4.1","r":"Mal.4.1","rawR":"Mal.4.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.4.1","value":"Mal.4.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:1"},")."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["Content"],"sourceClass":"h2","t":"p"},{"c":["God’s Great Love for Israel (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.1.1-5","r":"Mal.1.1-5","rawR":"Mal.1.1-5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.1.1-5","value":"Mal.1.1-5"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:1-5"},")"],"sourceClass":"h3","t":"p"},{"c":["To introduce the book, Malachi presents a contrast between God’s love for Israel and his hatred for Edom. Yet the assertion of God’s love is greeted with a strange question: “How have you loved us?” God loved Israel by entering into a covenant with the nation at Mt Sinai, just after he had freed them from the prison of Egypt. He had chosen them as his special people (cf. ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.12.1-3","r":"Gen.12.1-3","rawR":"Gen.12.1-3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.12.1-3","value":"Gen.12.1-3"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gn 12:1-3"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.19.5-6","r":"Exod.19.5-6","rawR":"Exod.19.5-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.19.5-6","value":"Exod.19.5-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ex 19:5-6"},"), whereas the descendants of Esau were not chosen (cf. ",{"href":"?bref=Rom.9.10-13","r":"Rom.9.10-13","rawR":"Rom.9.10-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Rom.9.10-13","value":"Rom.9.10-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"Rom 9:10-13"},"). Both Israel and Edom endured invasion and destruction, but only Israel was restored and rebuilt after the exile. The people of Edom were driven from their homeland by the Nabateans between 550 and 400 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},", and they never regained their territory. Through the judgment of Edom, God demonstrated that he is the great Ruler over the nations (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.1.5","r":"Mal.1.5","rawR":"Mal.1.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.1.5","value":"Mal.1.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mal 1:5"},") and that he will not forget Israel."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["The Unacceptable Sacrifices of the Priests (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.1.6-14","r":"Mal.1.6-14","rawR":"Mal.1.6-14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.1.6-14","value":"Mal.1.6-14"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:6-14"},")"],"sourceClass":"h3","t":"p"},{"c":["Although God deserved the honor and reverence of the Israelites, both the people and the priests openly disdained his laws and regulations. Strangely, it was the priests who led the way into disobedience. Sacrifices and offerings were supposed to atone for sin, but the animals offered by the priests only served to pollute or defile the altar (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.1.7,12","r":"Mal.1.7,12","rawR":"Mal.1.7,12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.1.7,12","value":"Mal.1.7,12"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:7, 12"},"). According to Leviticus, animals with defects were unacceptable as sacrifices, but Malachi mentions that the priests were offering to the Lord animals that were stolen and mutilated, crippled and sick (v ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.1.13","r":"Mal.1.13","rawR":"Mal.1.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.1.13","value":"Mal.1.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"13"},"; cf. v ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.1.8","r":"Mal.1.8","rawR":"Mal.1.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.1.8","value":"Mal.1.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"8"},"). To emphasize their contempt, the Lord challenged the priests to bring comparable presents to the governor. Would they dare to insult him in this fashion and face sure rejection? Rather than having the priests continue to bring unfit sacrifices to the altar, the Lord asked them to close the temple doors entirely (v ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.1.10","r":"Mal.1.10","rawR":"Mal.1.10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.1.10","value":"Mal.1.10"}],"t":"ref","v":"10"},"). Going through the motions never pleased God, either in ancient times (cf. ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.1.12-13","r":"Isa.1.12-13","rawR":"Isa.1.12-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.1.12-13","value":"Isa.1.12-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"Is 1:12-13"},") or modern. By calling the altar and its sacrifices “contemptible” (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.1.7,12","r":"Mal.1.7,12","rawR":"Mal.1.7,12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.1.7,12","value":"Mal.1.7,12"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mal 1:7, 12"},"), the priests were no better than the wicked sons of Eli, whose disregard of the rules for sacrifices sent them to a premature death (cf. ",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.2.15-17","r":"1Sam.2.15-17","rawR":"1Sam.2.15-17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.2.15-17","value":"1Sam.2.15-17"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Sm 2:15-17"},")."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["In sharp contrast to the attitude of the priests stands the emphasis upon God’s greatness in ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.1.11","r":"Mal.1.11","rawR":"Mal.1.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.1.11","value":"Mal.1.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"Malachi 1:11"}," and 14. God is more powerful than the gods of other nations, and even if Israel’s priests and people dishonor the Lord, eventually pure offerings will be brought to God by believing Gentiles. Perhaps these offerings refer to prayer and praise (cf. ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.19.14","r":"Ps.19.14","rawR":"Ps.19.14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.19.14","value":"Ps.19.14"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 19:14"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Heb.13.15","r":"Heb.13.15","rawR":"Heb.13.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Heb.13.15","value":"Heb.13.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"Heb 13:15"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Rev.5.8","r":"Rev.5.8","rawR":"Rev.5.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Rev.5.8","value":"Rev.5.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"Rv 5:8"},"), but others interpret the reference more literally (cf. ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.56.7","r":"Isa.56.7","rawR":"Isa.56.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.56.7","value":"Isa.56.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"Is 56:7"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.60.7","r":"Isa.60.7","rawR":"Isa.60.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.60.7","value":"Isa.60.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"60:7"},"). Peter may be alluding to this verse in connection with the conversion of Cornelius (",{"href":"?bref=Acts.10.35","r":"Acts.10.35","rawR":"Acts.10.35","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Acts.10.35","value":"Acts.10.35"}],"t":"ref","v":"Acts 10:35"},")."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":["The Punishment of the Priests (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.2.1-9","r":"Mal.2.1-9","rawR":"Mal.2.1-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.2.1-9","value":"Mal.2.1-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:1-9"},")"],"sourceClass":"h3","t":"p"},{"c":["One of the functions of the priests was to pronounce blessings upon the people in the name of God, but their disgraceful behavior turned the blessings into curses (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.2.2","r":"Mal.2.2","rawR":"Mal.2.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.2.2","value":"Mal.2.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mal 2:2"},"). Because of the priests’ sinfulness and the poor condition of the animals, their sacrifices were also worthless, and the entrails of the animals will be spread on their faces as a sign that God holds them in contempt. The disgrace heaped upon the priests differs sharply from the honor enjoyed by Aaron and his descendants. Malachi refers to a covenant of life and peace (v ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.2.5","r":"Mal.2.5","rawR":"Mal.2.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.2.5","value":"Mal.2.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"5"},") made with Levi and more particularly with Aaron’s grandson Phinehas, who courageously took action against the Jews involved in idolatry and immorality (",{"href":"?bref=Num.25.10-13","r":"Num.25.10-13","rawR":"Num.25.10-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.25.10-13","value":"Num.25.10-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"Nm 25:10-13"},"). In those days the priests revered the Lord and turned many from sin (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.2.6","r":"Mal.2.6","rawR":"Mal.2.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.2.6","value":"Mal.2.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mal 2:6"},")."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["Another responsibility of the priests was to teach the nation the law handed down by Moses (cf. ",{"href":"?bref=Lev.10.11","r":"Lev.10.11","rawR":"Lev.10.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.10.11","value":"Lev.10.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lv 10:11"},"). Like prophets, they were messengers of the Lord (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.2.7","r":"Mal.2.7","rawR":"Mal.2.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.2.7","value":"Mal.2.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mal 2:7"},") who were supposed to walk close to the Lord, but now the priests disregarded the law and were dishonest in handing down judicial decisions (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.2.9","r":"Mal.2.9","rawR":"Mal.2.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.2.9","value":"Mal.2.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mal 2:9"},"; cf. ",{"href":"?bref=Lev.19.15","r":"Lev.19.15","rawR":"Lev.19.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.19.15","value":"Lev.19.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lv 19:15"},")."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":["The Unfaithfulness of the People (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.2.10-16","r":"Mal.2.10-16","rawR":"Mal.2.10-16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.2.10-16","value":"Mal.2.10-16"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:10-16"},")"],"sourceClass":"h3","t":"p"},{"c":["In light of the attitude of the priests, it is not surprising to discover that the people at large were unfaithful to the Lord. God had formed Israel to be his special people, but the people had broken faith with him. A major factor in their unfaithfulness was intermarriage with foreigners, a sin mentioned in ",{"href":"?bref=Ezra.9.1-2","r":"Ezra.9.1-2","rawR":"Ezra.9.1-2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ezra.9.1-2","value":"Ezra.9.1-2"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ezra 9:1-2"}," and ",{"href":"?bref=Neh.13.23-29","r":"Neh.13.23-29","rawR":"Neh.13.23-29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Neh.13.23-29","value":"Neh.13.23-29"}],"t":"ref","v":"Nehemiah 13:23-29"},". By marrying pagan women, the men of Israel invariably began to worship pagan gods and turn from the Lord. When such intermarriage occurred, it sometimes followed the divorce of an Israelite wife. In ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.2.14-15","r":"Mal.2.14-15","rawR":"Mal.2.14-15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.2.14-15","value":"Mal.2.14-15"}],"t":"ref","v":"Malachi 2:14-15"}," God underscores the sacred commitment that he himself witnesses when two people marry. If that marriage covenant is shattered by divorce, God is deeply displeased. And it is even more tragic if divorce became an excuse to marry a more attractive or appealing foreigner."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["The Coming of the Messenger of the Covenant (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.2.17-3.5","r":"Mal.2.17-3.5","rawR":"Mal.2.17-3.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.2.17-3.5","value":"Mal.2.17-3.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:17–3:5"},")"],"sourceClass":"h3","t":"p"},{"c":["The sins of the priests and the people did not go unnoticed, even though the nation doubted that God would take action (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.2.17","r":"Mal.2.17","rawR":"Mal.2.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.2.17","value":"Mal.2.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:17"},"). But the third chapter opens with the announcement that the messenger of the covenant will indeed come to his temple. His way will be prepared by another messenger—a prophecy of John the Baptist, who prepared the way for the ministry of Christ (cf. ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.11.10","r":"Matt.11.10","rawR":"Matt.11.10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.11.10","value":"Matt.11.10"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mt 11:10"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Mark.1.2-3","r":"Mark.1.2-3","rawR":"Mark.1.2-3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mark.1.2-3","value":"Mark.1.2-3"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mk 1:2-3"},"). When Christ came, he revealed his anger when he cleansed the temple (cf. ",{"href":"?bref=John.2.13-17","r":"John.2.13-17","rawR":"John.2.13-17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"John.2.13-17","value":"John.2.13-17"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jn 2:13-17"},") and denounced the scribes and Pharisees (cf. ",{"href":"?bref=John.9.39","r":"John.9.39","rawR":"John.9.39","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"John.9.39","value":"John.9.39"}],"t":"ref","v":"9:39"},"), but most of his purifying and refining work awaits the Second Coming. Someday the priests and Levites will bring acceptable sacrifices, as they did in the days of Moses and Phinehas (cf. ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.3-4","r":"Mal.3.3-4","rawR":"Mal.3.3-4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.3-4","value":"Mal.3.3-4"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mal 3:3-4"}," and ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.2.4-5","r":"Mal.2.4-5","rawR":"Mal.2.4-5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.2.4-5","value":"Mal.2.4-5"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:4-5"},"). Verse ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.5","r":"Mal.3.5","rawR":"Mal.3.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.5","value":"Mal.3.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"5 of chapter 3"}," broadens the scope of the judgment to include the whole nation, as sorcerers, adulterers, and those who oppress the poor are condemned."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["The Benefits of Faithful Tithing (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.6-12","r":"Mal.3.6-12","rawR":"Mal.3.6-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.6-12","value":"Mal.3.6-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:6-12"},")"],"sourceClass":"h3","t":"p"},{"c":["Another specific weakness of postexilic Judah was the failure of the people to bring their tithes to the Lord. Encouraged by Nehemiah, the nation promised to tithe faithfully (cf. ",{"href":"?bref=Neh.10.37-39","r":"Neh.10.37-39","rawR":"Neh.10.37-39","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Neh.10.37-39","value":"Neh.10.37-39"}],"t":"ref","v":"Neh 10:37-39"},"), but apparently their good intentions were short-lived (cf. ",{"href":"?bref=Neh.13.10-11","r":"Neh.13.10-11","rawR":"Neh.13.10-11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Neh.13.10-11","value":"Neh.13.10-11"}],"t":"ref","v":"13:10-11"},"). According to ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.8-9","r":"Mal.3.8-9","rawR":"Mal.3.8-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.8-9","value":"Mal.3.8-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"Malachi 3:8-9"},", the tithes of the nation were so dismal that the people were, in effect, robbing God and were therefore under a curse. In verses ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.10-12","r":"Mal.3.10-12","rawR":"Mal.3.10-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.10-12","value":"Mal.3.10-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"10-12"}," Malachi challenges the nation to bring their tithes; then God would pour out his blessing upon them. Just as the opening of the “windows in heaven” meant the end of a famine in ",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.7.2,19","r":"2Kgs.7.2,19","rawR":"2Kgs.7.2,19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.7.2,19","value":"2Kgs.7.2,19"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kings 7:2, 19"},", so God promises that their crops will be so abundant that they will run out of storage space. The hope of “blessing” in ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.10","r":"Mal.3.10","rawR":"Mal.3.10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.10","value":"Mal.3.10"}],"t":"ref","v":"Malachi 3:10"}," and ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.12","r":"Mal.3.12","rawR":"Mal.3.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.12","value":"Mal.3.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"12"}," provides welcome relief from the curses mentioned in ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.1.14","r":"Mal.1.14","rawR":"Mal.1.14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.1.14","value":"Mal.1.14"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:14"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.2.2","r":"Mal.2.2","rawR":"Mal.2.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.2.2","value":"Mal.2.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:2"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.9","r":"Mal.3.9","rawR":"Mal.3.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.9","value":"Mal.3.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:9"},", and ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.4.6","r":"Mal.4.6","rawR":"Mal.4.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.4.6","value":"Mal.4.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:6"},"."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["The Day of the Lord (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.13-4.6","r":"Mal.3.13-4.6","rawR":"Mal.3.13-4.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.13-4.6","value":"Mal.3.13-4.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:13–4:6"},")"],"sourceClass":"h3","t":"p"},{"c":["Faced with the challenge of ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.10-12","r":"Mal.3.10-12","rawR":"Mal.3.10-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.10-12","value":"Mal.3.10-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"Malachi 3:10-12"},", the people of Israel responded in two different ways. One group denied that serving God brought any benefit (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.13-15","r":"Mal.3.13-15","rawR":"Mal.3.13-15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.13-15","value":"Mal.3.13-15"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:13-15"},"), while another segment of the nation bowed low before him with deep reverence (vv ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.16-18","r":"Mal.3.16-18","rawR":"Mal.3.16-18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.16-18","value":"Mal.3.16-18"}],"t":"ref","v":"16-18"},"). The unbelievers argued that obeying the Lord was useless and that arrogant and evil people were the ones who prospered. In response to their charge, Malachi noted that God would remember who the righteous were in the Day of Judgment. Although all of Israel was included in the promise made to Abraham, only those who genuinely believed would be God’s treasured possession (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.17","r":"Mal.3.17","rawR":"Mal.3.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.17","value":"Mal.3.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:17"},"; cf. ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.19.5","r":"Exod.19.5","rawR":"Exod.19.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.19.5","value":"Exod.19.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ex 19:5"},"), with their names written in the Book of Life (cf. ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.16","r":"Mal.3.16","rawR":"Mal.3.16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.16","value":"Mal.3.16"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mal 3:16"},"). As for the arrogant and evildoers, the Day of the Lord will consume them and they will have no survivors (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.4.1","r":"Mal.4.1","rawR":"Mal.4.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.4.1","value":"Mal.4.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:1"},"). Those who revere the Lord will enjoy spiritual and physical health under the blessing and protection of God, who is called the “sun of righteousness” (v ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.4.2","r":"Mal.4.2","rawR":"Mal.4.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.4.2","value":"Mal.4.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"2"},"). Like calves just released from confinement, the righteous will trample down the wicked and triumph over them (v ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.4.3","r":"Mal.4.3","rawR":"Mal.4.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.4.3","value":"Mal.4.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"3"},")."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["In view of the judgment associated with the Day of the Lord, Malachi urged the people to repent. To do this they needed to heed the law of Moses and take seriously the decrees and commands given at Mt Sinai (",{"href":"?bref=Mal.4.4","r":"Mal.4.4","rawR":"Mal.4.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.4.4","value":"Mal.4.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:4"},"; cf. ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.7","r":"Mal.3.7","rawR":"Mal.3.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.7","value":"Mal.3.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:7"},"). Just as Elijah called on Israel to turn back to God, so a new “Elijah” will preach repentance to a rebellious nation. When John the Baptist prepared the way for Christ (cf. ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.1","r":"Mal.3.1","rawR":"Mal.3.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.1","value":"Mal.3.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mal 3:1"},"), he ministered “in the spirit and power of Elijah” and begged the Jews to turn from their sin and humble themselves before God (",{"href":"?bref=Luke.1.17","r":"Luke.1.17","rawR":"Luke.1.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Luke.1.17","value":"Luke.1.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lk 1:17"},"). If they refused to listen, the nation faced the prospect of total destruction, the curse placed upon the people of Canaan (cf. ",{"href":"?bref=Josh.6.17-19","r":"Josh.6.17-19","rawR":"Josh.6.17-19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.6.17-19","value":"Josh.6.17-19"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jos 6:17-19"},") and upon the nation of Edom, whose collapse was described in ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.1.2-5","r":"Mal.1.2-5","rawR":"Mal.1.2-5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.1.2-5","value":"Mal.1.2-5"}],"t":"ref","v":"Malachi 1:2-5"},"."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":[{"c":["See also"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," ",{"href":"?item=IsraelHistoryof_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"IsraelHistoryof_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"IsraelHistoryof_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"Israel, History of"},"; ",{"href":"?item=PostexilicPeriod_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"PostexilicPeriod_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"PostexilicPeriod_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"Postexilic Period"},"; ",{"href":"?item=Prophecy_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"Prophecy_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"Prophecy_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"Prophecy"},"; ",{"href":"?item=ProphetProphetess_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"ProphetProphetess_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"ProphetProphetess_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"Prophet, Prophetess"},"."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"MalachiBookof","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"3:1","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.3.1","rawQueryValue":"Mal.3.1","sourceRefExact":"Mal.3.1"},{"anchorText":"2 Chr 36:15-16","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Chr.36.15-16","rawQueryValue":"2Chr.36.15-16","sourceRefExact":"2Chr.36.15-16"},{"anchorText":"Hg 1:13","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Hagg.1.13","rawQueryValue":"Hagg.1.13","sourceRefExact":"Hagg.1.13"},{"anchorText":"Neh 6:15","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Neh.6.15","rawQueryValue":"Neh.6.15","sourceRefExact":"Neh.6.15"},{"anchorText":"5:2-13","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Neh.5.2-13","rawQueryValue":"Neh.5.2-13","sourceRefExact":"Neh.5.2-13"},{"anchorText":"10:35-39","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Neh.10.35-39","rawQueryValue":"Neh.10.35-39","sourceRefExact":"Neh.10.35-39"},{"anchorText":"13:6-31","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Neh.13.6-31","rawQueryValue":"Neh.13.6-31","sourceRefExact":"Neh.13.6-31"},{"anchorText":"Nehemiah 13","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Neh.13.1-31","rawQueryValue":"Neh.13.1-31","sourceRefExact":"Neh.13.1-31"},{"anchorText":"Mal 1:6-14","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.1.6-14","rawQueryValue":"Mal.1.6-14","sourceRefExact":"Mal.1.6-14"},{"anchorText":"2:14-16","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.2.14-16","rawQueryValue":"Mal.2.14-16","sourceRefExact":"Mal.2.14-16"},{"anchorText":"3:8-11","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.3.8-11","rawQueryValue":"Mal.3.8-11","sourceRefExact":"Mal.3.8-11"},{"anchorText":"Malachi 1:8","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.1.8","rawQueryValue":"Mal.1.8","sourceRefExact":"Mal.1.8"},{"anchorText":"1:2","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.1.2","rawQueryValue":"Mal.1.2","sourceRefExact":"Mal.1.2"},{"anchorText":"2:17","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.2.17","rawQueryValue":"Mal.2.17","sourceRefExact":"Mal.2.17"},{"anchorText":"1:6","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.1.6","rawQueryValue":"Mal.1.6","sourceRefExact":"Mal.1.6"},{"anchorText":"3:14-18","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.3.14-18","rawQueryValue":"Mal.3.14-18","sourceRefExact":"Mal.3.14-18"},{"anchorText":"1:14","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.1.14","rawQueryValue":"Mal.1.14","sourceRefExact":"Mal.1.14"},{"anchorText":"5, 11","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.1.5,11","rawQueryValue":"Mal.1.5,11","sourceRefExact":"Mal.1.5,11"},{"anchorText":"2:10","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.2.10","rawQueryValue":"Mal.2.10","sourceRefExact":"Mal.2.10"},{"anchorText":"1:6","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.1.6","rawQueryValue":"Mal.1.6","sourceRefExact":"Mal.1.6"},{"anchorText":"3:5","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.3.5","rawQueryValue":"Mal.3.5","sourceRefExact":"Mal.3.5"},{"anchorText":"3:1","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.3.1","rawQueryValue":"Mal.3.1","sourceRefExact":"Mal.3.1"},{"anchorText":"Jn 2:14-15","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=John.2.14-15","rawQueryValue":"John.2.14-15","sourceRefExact":"John.2.14-15"},{"anchorText":"Mal 3:1-2","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.3.1-2","rawQueryValue":"Mal.3.1-2","sourceRefExact":"Mal.3.1-2"},{"anchorText":"2-4","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.3.2-4","rawQueryValue":"Mal.3.2-4","sourceRefExact":"Mal.3.2-4"},{"anchorText":"4:1","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.4.1","rawQueryValue":"Mal.4.1","sourceRefExact":"Mal.4.1"},{"anchorText":"1:1-5","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.1.1-5","rawQueryValue":"Mal.1.1-5","sourceRefExact":"Mal.1.1-5"},{"anchorText":"Gn 12:1-3","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Gen.12.1-3","rawQueryValue":"Gen.12.1-3","sourceRefExact":"Gen.12.1-3"},{"anchorText":"Ex 19:5-6","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Exod.19.5-6","rawQueryValue":"Exod.19.5-6","sourceRefExact":"Exod.19.5-6"},{"anchorText":"Rom 9:10-13","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Rom.9.10-13","rawQueryValue":"Rom.9.10-13","sourceRefExact":"Rom.9.10-13"},{"anchorText":"Mal 1:5","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.1.5","rawQueryValue":"Mal.1.5","sourceRefExact":"Mal.1.5"},{"anchorText":"1:6-14","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.1.6-14","rawQueryValue":"Mal.1.6-14","sourceRefExact":"Mal.1.6-14"},{"anchorText":"1:7, 12","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mal.1.7,12","rawQueryValue":"Mal.1.7,12","sourceRefExact":"Mal.1.7,12"},{"anchorText":"13","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","href